## PROJECT DESCRIPTION

- This is a monorepo that is used to build two web applications.
- One is called orderly and the other is called proclaim and both are created using vite-project as an entry point depending on the environment variable VITE_APP_NAME.
- There is another app called testing which is purely used for testing purposes.

# Packages:
- config: Configuration files shared across apps
- content: Application-specific content and components
- data: Data layer (Supabase integration as per rules)
- feature: Feature-specific implementations
- ui: Reusable UI components
- util: Utility functions

# Git

- never run git commands unless i specifically ask you to
- never create new branches
- when commiting code to git always push to remote
- use conventional commits specifications

# General Code Style & Formatting

- when i ask you to refactor a react component i want you to move any refactored code to a new file that is inside a folder that is inside a folder called components that is a sibling of the current file.
- do not create index files to export components
- Use English for all code and documentation.
- Always declare the type of each variable and function (parameters and return value).
- Avoid using any.
- Create necessary types.
- Use JSDoc to document public classes and methods.
- Don't leave blank lines within a function.
- One export per file.
- Use named exports instead of default exports. 

# Styling & UI

- Use Ionic Framework for styling.

# Data Fetching & Forms

- Use TanStack Query (react-query) for frontend data fetching.

# State Management & Logic

- Use Zustand for state management.

# Backend & Database

- Use Supabase for database access.

# Naming Conventions

- Use PascalCase for classes.
- Use camelCase for variables, functions, and methods.
- Use kebab-case for file and directory names.
- Use UPPERCASE for environment variables.
- Avoid magic numbers and define constants.
- Use snake_case for object property names

# Functions & Logic

- Keep functions short and single-purpose (<20 lines).
- Avoid deeply nested blocks by:
- Using early returns.
- Extracting logic into utility functions.
- Use higher-order functions (map, filter, reduce) to simplify logic.
- Use arrow functions for simple cases (<3 instructions), named functions otherwise.
- Use default parameter values instead of null/undefined checks.
- Use RO-RO (Receive Object, Return Object) for passing and returning multiple parameters.

# Data Handling

- Avoid excessive use of primitive types; encapsulate data in composite types.
- Avoid placing validation inside functions—use classes with internal validation instead.
- Prefer immutability for data:
- Use readonly for immutable properties.
- Use as const for literals that never change.


